{"name":"Blue Nile","hex":"#779fb9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Blue Nile","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river originating at Lake Tana in Ethiopia and flows into the Nile at Khartoum in Sudan; one of the two major tributaries of the Nile.","A state in southern Sudan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Blue_Nile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Nile","blocks":[{"text":"The Blue Nile is a river originating at Lake Tana in Ethiopia. It travels for approximately 1,450 km (900 mi) through Ethiopia and Sudan. Along with the White Nile, it is one of the two major tributaries of the Nile and supplies about 85.6% of the water to the Nile during the rainy season."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/ET_Bahir_Dar_asv2018-02_img32_view_from_Bezawit.jpg/330px-ET_Bahir_Dar_asv2018-02_img32_view_from_Bezawit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Nile"},{"title":"Nile","blocks":[{"text":"The Nile is a major north-flowing river in northeast Africa which empties into the Mediterranean Sea. At 7,088 kilometers (4,404 mi) long, it is the longest river in the world, although the volume of water it carries is much smaller than other major rivers such as the Amazon or the Congo. The Nile has played a central role in the environmental, economic, and cultural history of Africa for millennia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg/330px-Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Whiteandblueniles.jpg/500px-Whiteandblueniles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Whiteandblueniles.jpg/500px-Whiteandblueniles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Satellite image of where White and Blue Niles merge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhiteandblueniles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Zusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG/500px-Zusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Zusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG/1280px-Zusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Zusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG/500px-Zusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Confluence of the Blue and White Nile rivers near Khartoum, the capital of Sudan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZusammenfluss_der_Nile.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Completed_new_Sebara_Dildiy_bridge.jp